A WhatsApp-level video calling system is **far beyond a simple WebRTC demo**—it’s a globally distributed, low-latency, highly resilient real-time communication platform.

I’ll break this into a **real production architecture** used by apps like WhatsApp, Zoom, and Google Meet.

---

# 🧠 1. High-Level Architecture

```
Client (Mobile/Web)
   ↓
Signaling Layer (WebSocket / gRPC)
   ↓
Call Control Service
   ↓
Media Layer (WebRTC)
   ↓
STUN / TURN / SFU
   ↓
Observability + Scaling + Security
```

---

# 📡 2. Core Components

## 🔹 1. Signaling Layer (Go)

Purpose:

* Call setup
* SDP exchange
* ICE candidates
* Call state (ringing, accepted, ended)

### Tech

* Go
* Gorilla WebSocket or gRPC streams

### Responsibilities

* User presence (online/offline)
* Push call notifications
* Route messages between peers

👉 In production:

* Stateless services
* Behind load balancer
* Redis pub/sub for fanout

---

## 🔹 2. Call Control Service

This is the **brain**.

Handles:

* Call sessions
* Room creation
* Participant state
* Retry / reconnect logic

### Stack

* Go / Node.js / Java
* Redis (fast state)
* PostgreSQL (persistent logs)

---

## 🔹 3. Media Layer (WebRTC Engine)

Uses:

* WebRTC
* Pion WebRTC (Go backend)
* Native WebRTC (mobile apps)

Handles:

* Audio/video capture
* Encoding (VP8, H264)
* Encryption (DTLS-SRTP)

---

# 🌍 4. NAT Traversal (Critical)

Without this, calls fail on mobile networks.

## STUN

* Public IP discovery

## TURN

* Relay when P2P fails

### Use:

* coturn

👉 WhatsApp uses **massive TURN infrastructure globally**

---

# 🎥 5. Media Routing Strategy

## Option A: P2P (WhatsApp 1:1 calls)

✔ Low latency
✔ End-to-end encrypted
❌ Fails on strict NAT
❌ Not scalable for groups

---

## Option B: SFU (modern systems)

Use:

* mediasoup
* Janus WebRTC Server
* LiveKit

✔ Scales to many users
✔ Efficient bandwidth
✔ Adaptive bitrate

👉 WhatsApp group calls use **SFU-like architecture**

---

# 🔐 6. Security (WhatsApp-level)

## End-to-End Encryption (E2EE)

* DTLS + SRTP encryption
* Additional layer (WhatsApp style):

  * Signal Protocol

✔ Server cannot read media
✔ Keys exchanged via signaling

---

## Authentication

* JWT tokens
* Device binding
* Secure WebSocket (`wss://`)

---

